<section class="section thought-workflow"><div class="container"><div class="section-heading narrow-heading" data-reveal><div><span class="eyebrow">From experience to a published view</span><h2>The point is not to react faster. It is to understand better.</h2></div><p>A thought can begin anywhere. The discipline is in what happens before it becomes a conclusion.</p></div><ol class="thought-path" aria-label="How a thought develops"><li data-reveal><span>01</span><div><strong>Experience</strong><h3>Notice what does not quite add up.</h3><p>A project, journey, conversation, success or contradiction creates the first question.</p></div></li><li data-reveal><span>02</span><div><strong>Context</strong><h3>Follow the system around it.</h3><p>Look at people, history, incentives, markets, institutions, land and infrastructure — not only the visible event.</p></div></li><li data-reveal><span>03</span><div><strong>Evidence</strong><h3>Pressure-test the first answer.</h3><p>Compare what experience suggests with data, trade-offs and credible competing explanations.</p></div></li><li data-reveal><span>04</span><div><strong>View</strong><h3>Publish clearly, then remain revisable.</h3><p>State the conclusion I can defend now and leave the door open for stronger evidence later.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>
